WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:07.458
A circle of rocks in a field in southern England, near the city of Salisbury, has intrigued many different people for thousands of years.

2
00:00:07.459 --> 00:00:11.498
It is called Stonehenge, and it is the world's most famous megalith.

3
00:00:11.499 --> 00:00:20.504
Some of the stones used to make this megalith are over six meters tall; its massive size and old age have contributed to its fame throughout the world.

4
00:00:20.505 --> 00:00:28.662
But it is the mystery of its construction, and the many legends that surround it, that make Stonehenge the earth's most visited stone monument.

5
00:00:28.663 --> 00:00:36.885
Even after years of historical research and scientific investigation, there is still no clear understanding of how or why Stonehenge was built.

6
00:00:36.886 --> 00:00:41.989
Engineers ponder, "How exactly were these huge rocks transported and arranged?"

7
00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:47.827
Archaeologists ask, "What did this circle of rocks mean for ancient civilizations?"

8
00:00:47.828 --> 00:00:54.398
Investigators of the supernatural wonder, "Was this the site of a pagan temple, or a marker for alien visitors?"

9
00:00:54.399 --> 00:01:02.606
Many people have developed theories about Stonehenge─its origin and its purpose─but so far none of these theories have been proven correct.

10
00:01:02.607 --> 00:01:08.584
Stonehenge is made up of several concentric circles of stones that are surrounded by a circular ditch.

11
00:01:08.585 --> 00:01:17.277
The sheer size of the stones and their imposing arrangement make Stonehenge far more sophisticated than any other building or monument built in Britain at the time.

12
00:01:17.278 --> 00:01:30.055
All of the larger stones once had horizontal stones balanced on top of them, but many of these stones have fallen down or been damaged over the centuries─some have even been removed to build nearby homes and bridges.

13
00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:35.419
The construction of Stonehenge began around 3100 BC and took place in three stages.

14
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:39.105
The indigenous people of England were the first to work on Stonehenge.

15
00:01:39.106 --> 00:01:43.455
These were ancient farmers who had a deep reverence for circles and symmetry.

16
00:01:43.456 --> 00:01:49.772
They dug a circular ditch about one hundred meters in diameter and built an embankment just inside it.

17
00:01:49.773 --> 00:01:55.717
They made two entrances in the ditch: a large one, with an avenue leading away from it, and a small one.

18
00:01:55.718 --> 00:02:00.091
They then dug 56 evenly spaced holes within the giant circle.

19
00:02:00.092 --> 00:02:05.707
These holes probably held wooden posts at one time, but those disappeared long ago.

20
00:02:05.708 --> 00:02:09.986
Work on Stonehenge started again in about 2500 BC.

21
00:02:09.987 --> 00:02:15.474
This time between sixty and eighty stones were arranged in two circles in the middle of the circle.

22
00:02:15.475 --> 00:02:21.616
The relatively small stones that were used are called bluestones because they look blue when they get wet.

23
00:02:21.617 --> 00:02:27.923
The four-ton bluestones originally came from mountains in Wales, which are 380 kilometers away.

24
00:02:27.924 --> 00:02:36.443
Transporting these stones all this way was an astonishing feat, considering their size and the fact that most of the surrounding area was forested.

25
00:02:36.444 --> 00:02:44.854
The stones were probably dragged down to the sea, floated around the coast and up rivers on huge barges, and then dragged overland.

26
00:02:44.855 --> 00:02:50.169
After the bluestones were put in place, work stopped on Stonehenge for a few hundred years.

27
00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:56.334
Then, in 2300 BC, the third and final stage of Stonehenge's construction began.

28
00:02:56.335 --> 00:03:04.106
Thirty enormous sandstone rocks were arranged in a large circle, within the ring of post holes but around the outer ring of bluestones.

29
00:03:04.108 --> 00:03:08.643
Each weighed around 28 tons and measured up to 9 meters high.

30
00:03:08.644 --> 00:03:13.251
It is estimated that it would take six hundred men to move just one of these stones!

31
00:03:13.252 --> 00:03:22.156
Holes were dug two meters deep for the stones to sit in, and the stones were probably pulled into an upright position with the help of ropes and wooden frames.

32
00:03:22.157 --> 00:03:29.385
Then, ten more sandstone blocks were set at the center of the site in five pairs, with large rocks balanced on top of them.

33
00:03:29.386 --> 00:03:32.382
These stones were arranged in the shape of a horseshoe.

34
00:03:32.383 --> 00:03:39.497
Finally, a pointed stone called the Heel Stone was placed at the beginning of the avenue extending from the large entrance.

35
00:03:39.498 --> 00:03:43.685
When it was completed, the site was a magnificent thing to behold.

36
00:03:43.686 --> 00:03:48.648
People have had a lot of time to wonder about Stonehenge, since it was built thousands of years ago.

37
00:03:48.649 --> 00:03:52.921
Its size and unique construction have inspired many legends about its origin.

38
00:03:52.922 --> 00:03:59.440
According to one legend, Stonehenge was created when a group of giants dancing in a circle suddenly turned to stone.

39
00:03:59.441 --> 00:04:06.998
Many people who know this legend do say that Stonehenge's massive stones somewhat resemble the shape of giants holding hands.

40
00:04:06.999 --> 00:04:11.428
Others believe that Stonehenge owes its existence to the magician Merlin.

41
00:04:11.429 --> 00:04:19.472
A bloody battle was fought between the British and the Saxons on Salisbury Plain, the location of Stonehenge, in 450 AD.

42
00:04:19.473 --> 00:04:25.045
After the battle the British king wanted a monument for his soldiers that had been slain in the battle.

43
00:04:25.046 --> 00:04:33.577
So, according to the legend, his magician, Merlin, transported a stone circle, which was called the Giant's Ring, from Ireland to Britain.

44
00:04:33.578 --> 00:04:36.747
There are even more theories about Stonehenge's purpose.

45
00:04:36.748 --> 00:04:40.471
Some people believe that it is a signpost for visiting UFOs.

46
00:04:40.472 --> 00:04:44.175
Stonehenge might have another relationship with outer space as well.

47
00:04:44.176 --> 00:04:48.805
Some people believe that the stones correspond to the positions of the planets and moons.

48
00:04:48.806 --> 00:04:53.045
As a result they think that Stonehenge was used to predict eclipses.

49
00:04:53.046 --> 00:04:59.642
Most archaeologists agree that the stones were deliberately arranged to coincide with points in the sun's annual path.

50
00:04:59.643 --> 00:05:10.406
On the summer solstice, the sun's first rays shine directly into the center of the monument between the stones that form the central horseshoe, as the sun rises directly over the Heel Stone.

51
00:05:10.407 --> 00:05:16.026
Most experts believe that this phenomenon is too perfectly calculated to be an accident.

52
00:05:16.027 --> 00:05:25.144
This leads many to believe that Stonehenge was built as a site for the celebration of summer and winter solstices, which were very important times of the year for ancient cultures.

53
00:05:25.145 --> 00:05:31.222
Another theory suggests that ancient circles like Stonehenge may have originally been enclosures for cattle.

54
00:05:31.223 --> 00:05:38.027
Remains of fences and gates found at Coupland Henge, which is eight hundred years older than Stonehenge, support this idea.

55
00:05:38.028 --> 00:05:48.961
The theory states that cattle were moved into the enclosure during certain seasons; then, as time wore on, structures like Coupland Henge and Stonehenge began to be used for purely religious purposes.

56
00:05:48.962 --> 00:05:55.496
But until archaeologists can ascertain the true origin of Stonehenge, legends about it will continue.

57
00:05:55.497 --> 00:06:03.161
One difficulty archaeologists face is that the ancient people who lived on or visited Stonehenge left very little garbage.

58
00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:10.035
Studying trash is one of the best ways archaeologists can use to try to understand the life and habits of an ancient society.

59
00:06:10.036 --> 00:06:16.602
The lack of garbage at Stonehenge has led some archaeologists to conclude that Stonehenge was sacred ground.

60
00:06:16.603 --> 00:06:21.904
As one scientist put it, Stonehenge was a "clearly special place where you didn't litter."

61
00:06:21.905 --> 00:06:31.586
Stonehenge is the most intact and elaborate monument of its kind, but the remains of over a thousand stone circles exist throughout Britain and northern France.

62
00:06:31.588 --> 00:06:37.986
Some of them are small, like Keel Cross in County Cork, Ireland, which is only three meters in diameter.

63
00:06:37.987 --> 00:06:44.359
The largest stone circle is in Avebury, England; it covers over 28 acres and encircles a whole village.

64
00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:49.815
So, though Stonehenge is the center of many legends and mysteries, it is far from unique.

65
00:06:49.816 --> 00:06:56.734
Today Stonehenge is a UNESCO World Heritage Site that attracts 750,000 tourists each year.

66
00:06:56.735 --> 00:07:03.247
Recently people have been complaining about the cars, buses, and trucks that thunder past it on nearby roads.

67
00:07:03.248 --> 00:07:09.750
These annoyances have resulted in plans to remove the roads and restore the megalith's original grassland setting.

68
00:07:09.758 --> 00:07:17.711
In this peaceful environment, Stonehenge and the mystery of its origin and purpose will surely awe visitors for many years to come.

